  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Tied through six innings until Dom\u00ednguez made it matter late.",
    "what_this_means": "A pitcher who allows one hit in five scoreless frames doesn't lose many games. Fried's line was pristine. Seven strikeouts, no runs, nothing to work with for any offense. The problem wasn't the arm. The game sat at 0\u20130 for six full innings. That's not pitching dominance. That's mutual starvation. Dom\u00ednguez broke it in the seventh with a double, and Volpe drove him in. One swing changed everything because nothing else had. The bullpen closed it out clean. Chivilli, Blackburn, Warren, Hill all held. This is what happens when your starter is too good to lose but your offense is too quiet to win early. The Yankees won 2\u20130, but they didn't control this game. They survived it. They got out of Pittsburgh up one game in the loss column and still down 2.5 in the division, which means the doubleheader split was a wash and the night amounts to a single step. Late innings are where they're finding their wins right now. That's not a position to build on. That's a margin that gets thinner every time you ride it.",
    "what_to_watch": "Jes\u00fas Luzardo starts tomorrow in Philadelphia with a 3.43 ERA and a Phillies lineup that's been swinging it. Will Warren takes the ball for the Yankees. This is his kind of matchup, clean-up early, let the offense breathe.",
